From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xylar Asay-Davis <xylarstorm@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 11:44:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Pass a long to CURLOPT_POSTFIELDSIZE

CURLOPT_POSTFIELDSIZE is a long option, but this call site passed the bare
integer literal 0. libcurl's typecheck-gcc.h checks the argument type with
curlcheck_long(), which on LP64 platforms accepts only long/signed
long/unsigned long -- int is accepted solely when sizeof(long) ==
sizeof(int). The int literal therefore fails the check and expands to a call
to Wcurl_easy_setopt_err_long(), which carries
__attribute__((warning("curl_easy_setopt expects a long argument"))).

clang reports that as -Wattribute-warning, and the project builds with
-Werror on all UNIX platforms, so the build fails. Every other long option
in this file already passes a long (1L) or a long variable; this was the
only remaining int literal.

gcc does not emit the diagnostic and MSVC never includes typecheck-gcc.h, so
only clang-based builds (e.g. macOS) were affected.
---
 adapters/httpapi_curl.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/adapters/httpapi_curl.c b/adapters/httpapi_curl.c
index f654961..cba08bd 100644
--- a/adapters/httpapi_curl.c
+++ b/adapters/httpapi_curl.c
@@ -632,7 +632,7 @@ HTTPAPI_RESULT HTTPAPI_ExecuteRequest(HTTP_HANDLE handle, HTTPAPI_REQUEST_TYPE r
                                 if (requestType != HTTPAPI_REQUEST_GET)
                                 {
                                     if ((curl_easy_setopt(httpHandleData->curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, (void*)NULL) != CURLE_OK) ||
-                                        (curl_easy_setopt(httpHandleData->curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDSIZE, 0) != CURLE_OK))
+                                        (curl_easy_setopt(httpHandleData->curl, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDSIZE, 0L) != CURLE_OK))
                                     {
                                         result = HTTPAPI_SET_OPTION_FAILED;
                                         LogError("(result = %" PRI_MU_ENUM ")", MU_ENUM_VALUE(HTTPAPI_RESULT, result));
